---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const desc = 'Free data center power sizing calculator: turn rack count and per-rack draw into IT load, facility load at your PUE, and the utility capacity to reserve, with headroom for growth.';
const jsonld = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication',
  name: 'Data Center Power Sizing Calculator', applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
  operatingSystem: 'Web', offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
  url: 'https://theworldofai.org/calculators/power-sizing/',
});
---
<Base title="Data Center Power Sizing Calculator: racks to megawatts — The World of AI" description={desc} current="/calculators/" jsonld={jsonld}>
  <h1>Power Sizing Calculator</h1>
  <p class="answer">Facility power is IT load multiplied by PUE. IT load is rack count times
    average draw per rack, reduced by diversity because racks rarely peak together, then raised
    by whatever headroom you want for growth. An AI hall changes the arithmetic by changing one
    number: a general-purpose rack draws 5 to 10 kW, a dense GPU rack draws 40 to 130 kW, so the
    same floor can need ten times the utility service.</p>
  <p class="meta-line">Formula and defaults last reviewed: 2026-08-31.</p>

  <div class="calc">
    <label>Racks <input type="number" id="racks" value="200" min="1" step="1" /></label>
    <label>Average draw per rack (kW) <input type="number" id="perRack" value="12" min="0.1" step="0.5" /></label>
    <label>Diversity factor (%) <input type="number" id="div" value="85" min="10" max="100" step="5" /></label>
    <label>Growth headroom (%) <input type="number" id="growth" value="20" min="0" max="200" step="5" /></label>
    <label>PUE <input type="number" id="pue" value="1.3" min="1" max="3" step="0.01" /></label>
    <div class="out" id="out" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>

  <h2>How the math works</h2>
  <p>IT load = racks × kW per rack × diversity. Design IT load adds growth headroom on top.
    Facility load = design IT load × PUE, and that is the figure a utility interconnection
    request and a generator or UPS specification are sized against, not the IT number.</p>
  <p>Diversity below 100% reflects that nameplate draw and real draw differ. Training clusters
    are the exception worth knowing: a synchronised job can push a whole hall to near-peak at
    once, so for AI training capacity many operators design at or near 100% diversity.</p>

  <h2>What this does not tell you</h2>
  <p>Utility service is sized in kVA, not kW, so a power factor below unity raises the service
    above the number here. Redundancy topology matters as much as load: 2N doubles the UPS and
    distribution you buy without changing IT load at all. And the grid, not the building, is
    usually the constraint on when capacity can actually be energised.</p>

  <div class="cite-box">Cite this tool: <code>"Power Sizing Calculator." The World of AI,
    theworldofai.org/calculators/power-sizing/.</code></div>

  <script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var ids = ['racks', 'perRack', 'div', 'growth', 'pue'];
    function n(i) { return parseFloat(document.getElementById(i).value) || 0; }
    function calc() {
      var it = n('racks') * n('perRack') * (n('div') / 100);
      var design = it * (1 + n('growth') / 100);
      var fac = design * n('pue');
      function f(v, d) { return v.toLocaleString(undefined, { maximumFractionDigits: d || 0 }); }
      document.getElementById('out').innerHTML =
        'IT load today: <b>' + f(it) + ' kW</b> (' + f(it / 1000, 2) + ' MW)<br>' +
        'Design IT load with headroom: <b>' + f(design) + ' kW</b><br>' +
        'Facility load to reserve: <b>' + f(fac) + ' kW</b> (' + f(fac / 1000, 2) + ' MW)<br>' +
        '<span class="sub">Cooling and losses account for ' + f(fac - design) + ' kW of that.</span>';
    }
    ids.forEach(function (i) {
      document.getElementById(i).addEventListener('input', calc);
      document.getElementById(i).addEventListener('change', calc);
    });
    calc();
  })();
  </script>
  <style>
    .calc { border: 1px solid #e6e4de; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; background: #fafaf6; margin: 1.4em 0; }
    .calc label { display: block; margin: 0 0 12px; font-size: 14px; color: #26262b; }
    .calc input, .calc select { display: block; margin-top: 4px; padding: 7px 9px; font-size: 15px;
      border: 1px solid #cfcdc6; border-radius: 6px; width: 220px; max-width: 100%; }
    .out { margin-top: 14px; padding-top: 14px; border-top: 1px solid #e6e4de; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.7; }
    .out b { color: #201868; }
    .sub { color: #7A8A9E; font-size: 13.5px; }
  </style>
</Base>
